+\begin{proof}[Proof of (b): mass and level change]
+Let $U'\le U\le\hat\cO^\times$ be open, put $X=\XB{U}$, $X'=\XB{U'}$, and let $\pi:X'\to X$, $[x']\mapsto[x']$ be the natural projection (forgetting $U'$ to $U$).  Fix representatives $x_i\in\Bhat^\times$ for $X$ and write $\Gamma_i=\Gamma_{x_i}(U)=B^\times\cap x_iUx_i^{-1}$.  For each $i$ the fibre $\pi^{-1}([x_i])$ is identified with the orbit set $\Gamma_i\backslash U/U'$.  Indeed $B^\times x_iU=\bigsqcup_{u\in U/U'}B^\times x_iuU'$ and two cosets $x_iu_1U',x_iu_2U'$ coincide in $X'$ iff $x_iu_1=\gamma x_iu_2u'$ for some $\gamma\in B^\times$, $u'\in U'$, i.e.\ $\gamma=x_iu_1u'^{-1}u_2^{-1}x_i^{-1}\in\Gamma_i$ and $u_1U'$ and $u_2U'$ lie in the same $\Gamma_i$-orbit on $U/U'$ via left multiplication after conjugating by $x_i$.
+
+Hence fix $i$.  The $\Gamma_i$-set $U/U'$ is finite of cardinality $[U:U']$ (compact-open).  Its orbits are the points $y=[x_iu]$ of the fibre, and for $y$ the stabiliser in $\Gamma_i$ is exactly $\Gamma_y(U')=B^\times\cap x_iuU'u^{-1}x_i^{-1}=\Gamma_i\cap x_iuU'u^{-1}x_i^{-1}$.  By orbit--stabiliser, if $\mathcal O_{i,y}$ denotes the $\Gamma_i$-orbit of $uU'$ corresponding to $y$, $|\mathcal O_{i,y}|=[\Gamma_i:\Gamma_y(U')]$.  Consequently
+\begin{equation*}
+\sum_{y\in\pi^{-1}([x_i])}\frac1{|\Gamma_y(U')|}
+ =\sum_{\text{orbits }\mathcal O}\frac1{|\Stab(\mathcal O)|}
+ =\sum_{\mathcal O}\frac{|\mathcal O|}{|\Gamma_i|}
+ =\frac{|U/U'|}{|\Gamma_i|}.
+\end{equation*}
+Indeed each orbit contributes $1/|\Stab|=|\mathcal O|/|\Gamma_i|$, and the sum of orbit sizes is $|U/U'|=[U:U']$.  Summing over $i$ gives
+\[
+\sum_{y\in X'}\frac1{|\Gamma_y(U')|}= [U:U']\sum_{[x]\in X}\frac1{|\Gamma_x(U)|},
+\]
+which is the mass level-change with $|\Gamma|$.  In Lean this is \texttt{mass\_orbit\_count} (\Tpath{P2/Mass.lean}).
+
+For $w_x=|\Gamma_x/(\Gamma_x\cap\{\pm1\})|$ recall $\Gamma_x\cap\{\pm1\}=\{\pm1\}$ if $-1\in U$ and $= \{1\}$ otherwise, because $-1\in B^\times$ is central and lies in $xUx^{-1}$ iff $-1\in U$.  Hence
+\[
+w_x=\begin{cases}
+|\Gamma_x|/2 & -1\in U,\\
+|\Gamma_x|   & -1\notin U.
+\end{cases}
+\]
+If $-1\in U'$ then $-1\in U$ (since $U'\le U$), so both sides use $|\Gamma|/2$ and the same computation gives
+\[
+\sum_{X'}1/w_{x'}=[U:U']\sum_X1/w_x .
+\]
+If $-1\notin U$ then both sides use $|\Gamma|$ and the same holds.  These are the cases $-1\in U'$ or $-1\notin U$.  If $-1\in U\setminus U'$ then $w_x=|\Gamma_x|/2$ but $w_{x'}=|\Gamma_{x'}|$, whence $1/w_{x'}=2/|\Gamma_{x'}|$ while $1/w_x=2/|\Gamma_x|/2$?  More directly,
+\[
+\sum_{X'}1/w_{x'}=\sum_{X'}1/|\Gamma_{x'}|
+ =[U:U']\sum_X1/|\Gamma_x|
+ =[U:U']\sum_X2/w_x
+ =2[U:U']\sum_X1/w_x,
+\]
+and conversely $\sum_{X'}1/|\Gamma_{x'}|=\tfrac12\sum_{X'}1/w_{x'}$ in this case.  Thus with $w$ the formula holds without correction iff $-1\in U'$ or $-1\notin U$, and otherwise acquires the stated factor $2$.  This factor is visible already for $U=\hat\cO^\times$, $U'=\Uone{N}$ ($N\ge3$): $-1\in U\setminus U'$.
+\end{proof}
