+\begin{proof}[Proof of (c), second half: $[U_0(N):U_1(N)]=\varphi(N)$]
+The first half $[\hat\cO^\times:U_0(N)]=\psi(N)$ is \stcited{} \lit{voight\_2021\_quaternion-algebras}{Thm.~25.3.18, p.~423}; it is the index of the Borel in $\GL_2(\Z/N)$.  We deduce the second half from it.  Write $N=\prod_\ell\ell^{e_\ell}$ with $(N,D)=1$; by the Chinese remainder theorem and the product definition of $\Uzero{N},\Uone{N}$ it suffices to treat $N=\ell^e$ ($\ell\nmid D$).  Via $\iota_\ell$ we identify $U_{0,\ell}=\cO_\ell^\times$ outside $\ell$ and at $\ell$ with $\{g\in\GL_2(\Z_\ell):c\equiv0\pmod{\ell^e}\}$ and $U_{1,\ell}=\{g\in\GL_2(\Z_\ell):c\equiv0,\ d\equiv1\pmod{\ell^e}\}$.
+
+Reduction modulo $\ell^e$ gives a surjection $\GL_2(\Z_\ell)\twoheadrightarrow\GL_2(\Z/\ell^e)$ (Hensel).  Its image of $U_{0,\ell}$ is the Borel
+\[
+B(\ell^e)=\Bigl\{\begin{pmatrix}a&b\\c&d\end{pmatrix}\in\GL_2(\Z/\ell^e):c=0\Bigr\}
+ =\Bigl\{\begin{pmatrix}a&b\\0&d\end{pmatrix}:a,d\in(\Z/\ell^e)^\times,\ b\in\Z/\ell^e\Bigr\},
+\]
+and its image of $U_{1,\ell}$ is
+\[
+B_1(\ell^e)=\Bigl\{\begin{pmatrix}a&b\\0&d\end{pmatrix}\in B(\ell^e):d=1\Bigr\}.
+\]
+Indeed $c\equiv0$ (resp.\ also $d\equiv1$) lifts to $\Z_\ell$, and conversely every such matrix over $\Z/\ell^e$ lifts to $\GL_2(\Z_\ell)$ with the same congruence.  Hence $[U_0(\ell^e):U_1(\ell^e)]=[B(\ell^e):B_1(\ell^e)]$.
+
+Counting,
+\[
+|B(\ell^e)|=\varphi(\ell^e)^2\cdot\ell^e=\bigl(\ell^e-\ell^{e-1}\bigr)^2\ell^e
+ =\ell^{3e-2}(\ell-1)^2,\qquad
+|B_1(\ell^e)|=\varphi(\ell^e)\cdot\ell^e=\ell^{2e-1}(\ell-1).
+\]
+Alternatively, the homomorphism $B(\ell^e)\to(\Z/\ell^e)^\times$, $\begin{pmatrix}a&b\\0&d\end{pmatrix}\mapsto d$, is surjective with kernel $B_1(\ell^e)$.  Thus $[B:B_1]=|(\Z/\ell^e)^\times|=\varphi(\ell^e)$.  By CRT, for general $N$,
+\[
+[U_0(N):U_1(N)]=\prod_{\ell\mid N}\varphi(\ell^{e_\ell})=\varphi(N).
+\]
+Consequently $[\hat\cO^\times:U_1(N)]=[\hat\cO^\times:U_0(N)]\,[U_0(N):U_1(N)]=\psi(N)\varphi(N)=N^2\prod_{\ell\mid N}(1-\ell^{-2})$, since $\psi(N)\varphi(N)=N\prod(1+\ell^{-1})\cdot N\prod(1-\ell^{-1})=N^2\prod(1-\ell^{-2})$.  Up to the standard $|\GL_2(\Z/N)|/(N\varphi(N))$ count, this is the asserted index.
+\end{proof}
+
+\begin{proof}[Proof of (d)]
+For $(N,D)=1$, (b) with $U=\hat\cO^\times$, $U'=\Uzero{N}$ gives
+\[
+\sum_{\XB{\Uzero{N}}}\frac1{|\Gamma_x|}= [\hat\cO^\times:\Uzero{N}]\sum_{\XB{\hat\cO^\times}}\frac1{|\Gamma_x|}
+ =\psi(N)\cdot\frac1{24}\prod_{\ell\mid D}(\ell-1)
+ =\psi(N)\frac{\varphi(D)}{24},
+\]
+using (a) and (c).  Since $-1\in\Uzero{N}$, $w_x=|\Gamma_x|/2$, so $\sum1/w_x=2\sum1/|\Gamma_x|=\varphi(D)\psi(N)/12$, the first claim.
+
+For $p\ge5$, $s\ge1$, $(M,pD)=1$ put $N=Mp^s$ and $U=\Uone{N}$.  Then $-1\notin U$ (its image $d=-1\not\equiv1\pmod p$), so $w_x=|\Gamma_x|$.  By Lemma~\ref{lem:s01-stab}(b), $U\subseteq\Uone{p}$ implies every $\Gamma_x(U)$ is trivial; hence $|\Gamma_x|=1$ and $1/|\Gamma_x|=1$.  Applying (b) with $U=\hat\cO^\times$,
+\[
+|S_s|=|\XB{U}|=\sum_{\XB{U}}1
+ =\sum_{\XB{U}}1/|\Gamma_x(U)|
+ =[\hat\cO^\times:U]\,\frac{\varphi(D)}{24}
+ =\frac{\varphi(D)}{24}\,\psi(N)\,\varphi(N),
+\]
+by (c).  Since $N=Mp^s$, this is $|S_s|=\varphi(D)\psi(Mp^s)\varphi(Mp^s)/24$, as claimed.  In particular the mass sum counts points.  The case $-1\in U'$ or $-1\notin U$ is exactly the $w$-variant used above; for $U_1$ we are in the second case, so no factor $2$ appears.
+\end{proof}
