 \begin{example}[checks]\label{ex:s01-masschecks}
-$D=2$: $\cO$ Hurwitz, $|\cO^\times|=24$, mass $1/24$; $|\XB{\Uone{5}}|=24/24=1$ (the $24$ nonzero vectors of $\F_5^2$
-form one free orbit of $\cO^\times$). $D=3$: mass $1/12$, $|S_s|=\frac{2}{24}\cdot24\cdot25^{s-1}=2\cdot25^{s-1}$ for
-$p=5$, $M=1$ --- this is the count used in seed test T3. $D=11$: $h=2$, $|\Gamma_x|\in\{4,6\}$, $\frac14+\frac16=\frac{10}{24}$.
-\stproved.
+We verify the three numerical checks required for C2.
+
+\emph{$D=2$: Hurwitz order.}  The unique maximal order $\cO$ (Hurwitz) has $|\cO^\times|=24$ (binary tetrahedral).  The class number at maximal level is $1$, so $\XB{\hat\cO^\times}=\{[1]\}$ with $|\Gamma_{[1]}|=24$.  Hence $\sum1/|\Gamma_x|=1/24=\frac1{24}\prod_{\ell\mid2}(\ell-1)$, i.e.\ mass $1/24$ (and $\sum1/w_x=1/12$ since $-1\in\hat\cO^\times$, $w_x=12$).  For $U=\Uone{5}$ we have $[\hat\cO^\times:U]=\psi(5)\varphi(5)=6\cdot4=24$ by (c).  The reduction map $\hat\cO^\times\to\GL_2(\F_5)$ identifies $U\backslash\hat\cO^\times$ with the $24$ non-zero vectors of $\F_5^2$?  More concretely, $|\XB{\Uone{5}}|=[\hat\cO^\times:U]\cdot1/24=1$; equivalently the $24$ non-zero vectors of $\F_5^2$ form one free orbit of $\cO^\times/\{\pm1\}$ (hence of $\cO^\times$ on $\mathbf P^1$ data).  Thus $|\XB{\Uone{5}}|=24/24=1$.  This is the $D=2$ check.
+
+\emph{$D=3$: $p=5$, $M=1$.}  Here $\varphi(D)=2$, mass $1/24\cdot2=1/12$.  For $s\ge1$, $N=p^s=5^s$, $\psi(N)=6\cdot5^{s-1}$, $\varphi(N)=4\cdot5^{s-1}$, so by (d)
+\[
+|S_s|=\frac{2}{24}\cdot24\cdot25^{s-1}=2\cdot25^{\,s-1},
+\]
+i.e.\ $|S_1|=2$, $|S_2|=50$, $|S_3|=1250$, etc.  This is the count used in seed test T3 ($D=3$, $p=5$: $|S_s|=2\cdot25^{s-1}$).  Explicitly $|S_1|=\frac{\varphi(3)}{24}\psi(5)\varphi(5)=\frac2{24}\cdot6\cdot4=2$.
+
+\emph{$D=11$:}  $D=11$ prime, $\varphi(D)=10$, mass $(11-1)/24=10/24$.  The class set at maximal level has $h=2$, say $\XB{\hat\cO^\times}=\{[x_1],[x_2]\}$ with $|\Gamma_{x_1}|=4$, $|\Gamma_{x_2}|=6$ (the two maximal orders have unit groups $C_4$ and $C_6$; $4\mid24$, $6\mid24$).  Then
+\[
+\frac1{|\Gamma_{x_1}|}+\frac1{|\Gamma_{x_2}|}=\frac14+\frac16=\frac{10}{24},
+\]
+matching the mass formula.  With $w_x=|\Gamma_x|/2$ ($-1\in\hat\cO^\times$) this is $\sum1/w_x=1/2+1/3=5/12=\varphi(D)/12$.
+\stproved{}
 \end{example}
